        <!-- SECTION 3 -->
        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Types of Garage Floor Epoxy Coatings Explained
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Water-Based Epoxy (40–50% Solids)</h3>
          <p>
            The most common DIY garage floor product. Water-based epoxy kits are lower odor, easier to apply, and widely available at hardware stores. The trade-off is film thickness — water accounts for 50-60% of the wet product, which evaporates during cure and leaves a thinner coating than higher-solids alternatives. Expect <strong>3–5 years of service life</strong> in typical residential garages. Best for homeowners doing their first coating project.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">High-Solids Epoxy (70–80% Solids)</h3>
          <p>
            A meaningful step up from water-based products. High-solids formulas deposit more cured material per coat, resulting in better abrasion resistance and chemical protection. The increased solids content also provides better gap-filling over minor surface imperfections. These products require slightly more careful application but deliver significantly longer service life — typically <strong>7–12 years</strong> in residential use.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">100% Solids Epoxy</h3>
          <p>
            Professional-grade coating used in automotive shops, aircraft hangars, and industrial facilities. No water or solvents — 100% of the applied material becomes cured coating. A single coat achieves the thickness of three water-based coats. Demanding to apply (short pot life, strong odor, requires careful surface prep) but delivers <strong>15–25+ year service life</strong> in residential garages. The right choice for serious workshops and car enthusiasts.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Polycuramine (Polyurea/Urethane Hybrid)</h3>
          <p>
            Technically not epoxy but often marketed alongside it. Polycuramine chemistry produces a coating that's harder than standard epoxy with superior hot-tire resistance and UV stability. Rust-Oleum RockSolid is the dominant consumer product in this category. More expensive than water-based epoxy but addresses epoxy's two biggest failure modes: hot-tire pickup and yellowing from UV exposure.
          </p>

