        <!-- SECTION 4: How to Apply -->
        <div id="how-to-apply" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How to Apply Garage Floor Epoxy: The Complete DIY Guide
          </h2>
          <p>
            Garage floor epoxy is a project where the difference between success and failure is almost entirely preparation. The epoxy itself is relatively forgiving — but apply it over a dirty, oily, or unsealed floor and it will peel within months regardless of which product you chose.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 1: Check for Moisture</h3>
          <p>
            Before anything else, tape a 2-foot square of plastic sheeting to the bare floor and seal the edges with tape. Leave it for 24 hours. If moisture droplets form on the underside of the plastic, you have moisture vapor transmission — a condition that will cause most epoxy products to delaminate, often within weeks. Address the moisture source before proceeding; do not apply epoxy over a floor with active moisture issues.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 2: Degrease Thoroughly</h3>
          <p>
            Oil-contaminated concrete is the other primary cause of epoxy failure. Apply a commercial concrete degreaser (TSP substitute or Purple Power work well) to the entire floor, scrub with a stiff brush, and rinse thoroughly. Repeat on any area where vehicles have been parked. Old oil stains may require multiple applications. The floor must be completely oil-free — epoxy cannot bond through oil contamination.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 3: Acid Etch or Mechanical Grind</h3>
          <p>
            Concrete straight from the slab is too smooth for epoxy adhesion. You need to open the surface pores by either acid etching (muriatic acid or the included etching solution in most kits) or mechanical grinding with a diamond-cup grinder. Acid etching is adequate for most DIY projects on sound concrete. Mechanical grinding is required for previously sealed concrete, very dense concrete, or floors where acid etching fails the water-bead test. After etching, rinse completely and allow to dry for a minimum of 24 hours.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-6">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-amber-700 mb-2">The Water Bead Test</p>
            <p class="text-amber-900 text-sm">After etching and rinsing, sprinkle water on the floor. It should soak in immediately like water on a sponge. If it beads up, the surface hasn't been opened enough and epoxy won't bond properly. Re-etch or switch to mechanical grinding.</p>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 4: Mix and Apply the Epoxy</h3>
          <p>
            Mix Part A and Part B thoroughly according to the manufacturer's ratio (typically 1:1 or 2:1 by volume). Stir for a full 3 minutes — undermixed epoxy will have soft, uncured spots. Pour into a roller tray and apply with a 3/8-inch nap roller. Work in 10x10-foot sections, cutting in edges with a brush first. Maintain a wet edge to prevent lap marks. Apply thin, even coats rather than one thick coat.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 5: Broadcast Decorative Chips (Optional)</h3>
          <p>
            If your kit includes decorative color flakes, broadcast them into the wet epoxy immediately after rolling each section. Toss the chips underhand at roughly 4-foot height to achieve even distribution. Apply generously — unused chips can be swept up after curing. After the base coat cures, sweep and vacuum excess chips, then apply a clear epoxy topcoat to lock them in place.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 6: Allow Full Cure Before Use</h3>
          <p>
            Light foot traffic is typically safe after 12–24 hours. Vehicle parking requires a minimum of 72 hours for water-based systems or 24–36 hours for polycuramine products. Full chemical cure takes 7 days — do not expose the floor to harsh chemicals, hot tires, or heavy loads during this period.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Surface Prep Deep Dive -->
        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How to Prep a Garage Floor for Epoxy: The Make-or-Break Step
          </h2>
          <p>
            Every professional floor coating contractor will tell you the same thing: 80% of failures are preparation failures, not product failures. Here's what most DIYers get wrong.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">New Concrete Must Cure First</h3>
          <p>
            Freshly poured concrete continues to cure chemically for 28 days. Applying epoxy to concrete less than 28 days old risks adhesion failure as residual moisture and chemical activity prevent proper bonding. This is non-negotiable. Even if the surface looks dry and hard to the eye, the chemical curing process continues deep within the slab for the full 28-day period, and epoxy applied prematurely will fail regardless of how well you prepare the surface. Mark your calendar when the concrete is poured and do not schedule the epoxy project until after that date has passed.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Previously Painted or Sealed Floors</h3>
          <p>
            If your floor has ever been painted, sealed, or coated, you must either remove the existing coating entirely (grind it off) or apply a coating specifically designed to bond over existing finishes. Most standard epoxy kits will not adhere over previous coatings and will delaminate quickly if applied over them. When in doubt, do a quick adhesion test: apply a small amount of mixed epoxy to an inconspicuous area, let it cure for 24 hours, then try to peel it off. If it peels cleanly, the substrate is not ready and requires further preparation before proceeding with the full floor.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Cracks and Spalling</h3>
          <p>
            Fill all cracks wider than a hairline with a concrete crack filler or epoxy crack repair compound before coating. Cracks wider than 1/4 inch should be chased out (V-grooved with a grinder), filled, and allowed to cure before applying the floor coating. Trying to bridge cracks with epoxy alone results in the coating cracking in the same location.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Topcoat Options: Should You Apply a Clear Coat?</h3>
          <p>
            Many DIYers skip the topcoat step, but applying a clear urethane or polyaspartic topcoat over your epoxy base coat significantly extends the system's service life and dramatically improves scratch resistance. Epoxy is tough, but it's not particularly scratch-resistant — metal objects dragged across it, bicycle kickstands, and dropped tools leave marks that accumulate over years. A urethane topcoat is 3–4 times harder than epoxy and provides UV stability that prevents the yellowing common in epoxy-only systems.
          </p>
          <p>
            Polyaspartic topcoats are the premium option — they cure faster than urethane (often drive-on ready in 6–8 hours versus 24 hours), offer superior chemical resistance, and are 100% UV-stable with no yellowing over any time frame. The trade-off is cost: polyaspartic products run $60–90 per gallon versus $30–50 for quality urethane. For a workshop or showroom garage where appearance matters for years, the polyaspartic upgrade is worth it. For a utility garage, urethane delivers excellent performance at a reasonable price.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you apply a topcoat, do it within 24 hours of the base coat while the epoxy is still in its "green" (partially cured) state. Applying topcoat over fully cured epoxy requires scuff sanding the base coat first to create mechanical adhesion — a step that most instructions omit but that matters significantly for long-term inter-coat adhesion.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Decorative Color Flake Systems</h3>
          <p>
            The colored flake broadcast systems included in most consumer epoxy kits serve two purposes: they add decorative appeal, and they disguise minor surface imperfections and dirt between cleanings. The chips are broadcast into the wet base coat, allowed to cure, then locked in with a clear topcoat. The result is the distinctive speckled pattern seen in professional garage and commercial floors.
          </p>
          <p>
            Flake density is a personal choice. Light coverage (30–40% chip coverage) maintains a smooth feel underfoot and allows the epoxy color to show prominently. Full-broadcast coverage (70–100% chip density) creates a completely non-slip, highly decorative surface that hides the base coat color entirely. For garages that double as workshops or get heavy foot traffic, the additional grip of a higher chip density is a genuine safety benefit. For showroom-style car garages, the lower chip density with a smooth glossy topcoat is the more common aesthetic choice.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-6">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-red-700 mb-2">Common Mistakes That Ruin Epoxy</p>
            <ul class="space-y-2 text-red-900 text-sm">
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2"><svg class="w-4 h-4 text-red-500 mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M6 18L18 6M6 6l12 12"></path></svg>Applying to a damp floor (wait a full 24–48 hours after rain or washing)</li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2"><svg class="w-4 h-4 text-red-500 mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M6 18L18 6M6 6l12 12"></path></svg>Skipping the etch — smooth concrete has near-zero epoxy adhesion</li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2"><svg class="w-4 h-4 text-red-500 mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M6 18L18 6M6 6l12 12"></path></svg>Applying when temperatures are below 55°F or above 90°F</li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2"><svg class="w-4 h-4 text-red-500 mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M6 18L18 6M6 6l12 12"></path></svg>Parking hot vehicles before the full 7-day chemical cure</li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2"><svg class="w-4 h-4 text-red-500 mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M6 18L18 6M6 6l12 12"></path></svg>Under-mixing the two-part epoxy (leads to soft, tacky spots)</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <PlanTheJob />

        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
            Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does garage floor epoxy last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Quality garage floor epoxy lasts <strong>5–10 years</strong> with proper installation and maintenance. 100% solids commercial-grade epoxy can last 20+ years in light-use garages. Water-based DIY kits typically last 3–5 years before needing recoating. Proper surface preparation is the single biggest factor in longevity — the same product can last 2 years or 10 years depending on how the floor was prepped.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to etch my garage floor before applying epoxy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Yes, without exception. Acid etching or mechanical grinding opens the concrete pores and gives epoxy something to bite into. Smooth concrete has near-zero adhesion with epoxy — it will peel in sheets within weeks. Use the included etching solution from your kit, or a diluted muriatic acid solution (10:1 water to acid), and confirm adhesion readiness with the water bead test before applying any coating.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you apply garage floor epoxy yourself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Yes — DIY garage floor epoxy is highly achievable with proper preparation. Budget a <strong>full day for prep</strong> (degreasing, etching, rinsing, drying) and a <strong>half day for application</strong>. The prep day is always the more important one. Most project failures trace back to rushed or skipped preparation, not application mistakes.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does epoxy peel off garage floors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">The three main causes are: insufficient surface preparation (oil contamination or failure to etch), application over damp concrete (moisture vapor transmission), or applying over previously sealed concrete without proper stripping. Proper prep eliminates 95% of delamination failures. If your floor has had multiple coating failures, have a professional check for moisture vapor transmission before investing in another coating.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How long before I can park on epoxy garage floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Most water-based epoxy kits require <strong>72 hours</strong> before vehicle parking. 100% solids and polycuramine products are often drive-on ready in 24–36 hours. Always allow <strong>7 full days</strong> for complete chemical cure before exposing the coating to hot tires, heavy loads, or harsh chemicals. Parking a hot vehicle on insufficiently cured epoxy is the number one cause of tire mark failure in new floor coatings.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What temperature is best for applying garage floor epoxy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Both air and concrete surface temperatures should be between <strong>55°F and 90°F</strong>. Below 55°F, epoxy cures too slowly and may not bond properly. Above 90°F, it cures too fast — causing bubbles, lap marks, and uneven texture. The ideal application condition is a mild day (65–75°F) with low humidity and no rain in the 48-hour forecast.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you epoxy a garage floor yourself or should you hire a professional?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">DIY garage floor epoxy is achievable and cost-effective for most homeowners — the material cost for a two-car garage runs $150–400 for a quality kit, versus $1,500–4,000 for professional installation. The key requirement is thorough surface preparation: degreasing, etching or grinding, and moisture testing. Homeowners who are comfortable with careful prep work and can follow instructions closely achieve results that are virtually indistinguishable from professional installs. Hire a professional when you have active moisture vapor transmission issues, floors with multiple prior coating failures, or business/showroom applications where failure would be costly.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you maintain an epoxy garage floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Epoxy garage floors are low-maintenance but not zero-maintenance. Sweep or dust mop regularly to prevent abrasive grit from accumulating and scratching the surface under foot and vehicle traffic. Mop with a mild pH-neutral cleaner (avoid harsh acids or strong degreasers that can dull the finish over time). Clean up oil and chemical spills promptly — epoxy resists penetration but prolonged exposure to harsh chemicals can cause surface etching. Avoid using a hard plastic snow shovel directly on an epoxy floor in winter; a rubber-edged or foam-edged shovel prevents scratching. With basic care, an epoxy floor maintains its appearance for years and the only maintenance typically needed is occasional reapplication of a topcoat sealer every 3–5 years on high-traffic sections.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
